---
import { project, models, dimensions, tasks, steps } from '../data/paper';
import PaperCrop from '../components/PaperCrop.astro';
import '../styles/global.css';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const asset = (name: string) => `${base}assets/${name}`;
const paper = `${base}paper/figmaeval.pdf`;
const citation = `@misc{figmaeval,\n  title = {${project.title}},\n  author = {${project.authors.join(' and ')}},\n  howpublished = {${project.github}}\n}`;
const icons = {
  paper: 'M7 3h7l5 5v13H7z M14 3v6h5 M10 13h6 M10 17h6',
  github: 'M9 19c-4 1-4-2-5-2 M15 22v-4c0-1-.3-1.8-1-2 3-.3 6-1.5 6-6 0-1.4-.5-2.6-1.3-3.5.1-.5.5-2-.2-3.5 0 0-1.2-.4-3.8 1.3a13 13 0 0 0-7.4 0C4.7 2.6 3.5 3 3.5 3c-.7 1.5-.3 3-.2 3.5A5 5 0 0 0 2 10c0 4.5 3 5.7 6 6-.6.3-1 1-1 2v4',
  play: 'M9 5l11 7-11 7z',
  expand: 'M8 3H3v5 M16 3h5v5 M21 16v5h-5 M3 16v5h5',
};
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="description" content={project.description} />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#f8f9fc" />
    <meta property="og:title" content={project.title} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={project.description} />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:image" content={new URL(`${base}assets/open-design.png`, Astro.site).href} />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href={`${base}favicon.svg`} />
    <link rel="preload" as="image" href={asset('open-design.png')} />
    <link rel="canonical" href={new URL(base, Astro.site).href} />
    <title>{project.title}</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <a href="#main" class="skip-link">Skip to content</a>
    <header class="site-header">
      <a class="wordmark" href="#" aria-label="FigmaEval home"><img src={`${base}favicon.svg`} width="30" height="30" alt="" />Figma<span>Eval</span></a>
      <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
        <a href="#showcase">Showcase</a><a href="#benchmark">Benchmark</a><a href="#results">Results</a><a href="#paper">Paper</a>
      </nav>
      <a class="nav-code" href={project.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d={icons.github} /></svg><span>Code</span></a>
    </header>

    <main id="main">
      <section class="hero page-width" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <div class="eyebrow"><span class="tiny-grid" aria-hidden="true">✳</span> A BENCHMARK FOR DESIGN AGENTS</div>
          <h1 id="hero-title">Figma<span>Eval</span><span class="title-dot">.</span></h1>
          <p class="hero-headline">How well can AI agents<br />really <em>design?</em></p>
          <p class="hero-description">Open-ended briefs. Native Figma artifacts.<br class="desktop-break" /> Professional design quality, put to the test.</p>
          {project.authors.length > 0 && <p class="authors"><span>{project.authors.slice(0, 3).join(' · ')}</span><span>{project.authors.slice(3).join(' · ')}</span></p>}
          <div class="project-brand"><img src={asset('sensenova-logo.png')} alt="SenseNova" width="144" height="54" /></div>
          <div class="hero-actions">
            <a class="button button-primary" href={project.arxiv || paper} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d={icons.paper} /></svg>{project.arxiv ? 'Read on arXiv' : 'Read the paper'}</a>
            <a class="button button-white" href={project.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d={icons.github} /></svg>GitHub</a>
          </div>
          <a class="explore-link" href="#showcase"><span class="play-disc"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d={icons.play} /></svg></span>Explore the designs</a>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-stage" aria-label="Examples of AI-generated Figma designs featured in the paper">
          <div class="canvas-label"><span class="canvas-symbol" aria-hidden="true">⌘</span> THE DESIGN SPACE IS OPEN.</div>
          <div class="orbit orbit-one"></div><div class="orbit orbit-two"></div>
          <div class="hero-screen screen-back"><div class="screen-tag">Fitness <span>Explore</span></div><PaperCrop crop={tasks[1].crops[0]} alt="Claude Opus 5.5 fitness exploration screen from the paper" eager /></div>
          <div class="hero-screen screen-middle"><div class="screen-tag">MindfulMentor <span>Exam mode</span></div><PaperCrop crop={tasks[2].crops[0]} alt="Claude Opus 5.5 MindfulMentor screen from the paper" eager /></div>
          <div class="hero-screen screen-front"><div class="screen-tag">Foodgo <span>Customize</span></div><PaperCrop crop={tasks[0].crops[0]} alt="Claude Opus 5.5 burger customization screen from the paper" eager /></div>
          <div class="floating-note"><span class="note-icon" aria-hidden="true">◇</span><div>Built in Figma.<small>Native, editable design artifacts.</small></div></div>
          <div class="stage-caption"><span class="mini-swatch purple"></span> Actual agent outputs · Paper examples</div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="stats-band page-width" aria-label="Benchmark at a glance">
        {[['84','Design tasks'],['783','Required screens'],['8','Application domains'],['22','Expert-grounded rubrics'],['11','Evaluated models']].map(([value,label]) => <div class="stat"><strong>{value}</strong><span>{label}</span></div>)}
      </div>

      <section id="showcase" class="section showcase-section page-width">
        <div class="section-top"><div><p class="eyebrow">THE SAME BRIEF. DIFFERENT DESIGN DECISIONS.</p><h2>One task. Many possibilities.</h2></div><p class="section-aside">Requirements and resources are fixed.<br />The design solution is up to the agent.</p></div>
        <div class="task-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Design task">
          {tasks.map((task,i) => <button type="button" id={`tab-${task.id}`} role="tab" aria-selected={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'} aria-controls={`panel-${task.id}`} tabindex={i === 0 ? 0 : -1} data-task={i}>{task.name}</button>)}
        </div>
        {tasks.map((task,i) => <div id={`panel-${task.id}`} role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby={`tab-${task.id}`} class="showcase-panel" hidden={i !== 0}>
          <div class="brief-panel">
            <span class="label-chip">{task.category}</span><h3>{task.name}</h3><p class="screen-name">{task.screen}</p><p>{task.description}</p>
            <div class="brief-divider"></div><span class="small-label">DESIGN DECISIONS</span><p class="focus-text">{task.focus}</p>
            <div class="fixed-resource"><span aria-hidden="true">▤</span><div>Shared starter file<small>Brief · Foundations · Components · Media</small></div></div>
            <p class="source-caption">Selected screen excerpts from the paper.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="comparison-stage" style={`--task-color:${task.color}`}>
            {['Claude Opus 5.5','GPT-6.1 Sol'].map((model,index) => <figure class="design-result"><figcaption><span class={`mini-swatch ${index === 0 ? 'purple' : 'coral'}`}></span>{model}</figcaption><button class="screen-open" type="button" data-zoom aria-label={`Enlarge ${model} ${task.name} design`} data-caption={`${task.name} · ${task.screen} · ${model}`}><PaperCrop crop={task.crops[index]} alt={`${model} design for ${task.name}: ${task.screen}`} /><span class="enlarge-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d={icons.expand} /></svg></span></button></figure>)}
          </div>
        </div>)}
        <p class="under-caption">Agents decide information hierarchy, layout, components, asset composition, interaction states, and editable structure.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="benchmark" class="section benchmark-section">
        <div class="page-width">
          <div class="section-top"><div><p class="eyebrow">CONSTRAINED SPECIFICATION. OPEN SOLUTION.</p><h2>Real design starts with a brief.</h2></div><p class="section-aside">84 tasks from real-world Figma files.<br />A complete multi-screen deliverable for each task.</p></div>
          <div class="benchmark-story">
            <div class="story-copy"><h3>A shared starting point.<br />Room to make it your own.</h3><p>Each task specifies the product, required screens, functionality, content, and shared design resources. Reference layouts are withheld.</p><p>Agents work directly in Figma to make coherent, native design artifacts. The benchmark evaluates both the rendered interfaces and their underlying structure.</p><a class="text-link" href={paper} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the task formulation in the paper</a></div>
            <div class="starter-file"><div class="file-toolbar"><span class="file-mark">F</span> FigmaEval starter file <span class="readonly-tag">Read only</span></div><div class="starter-pages">{[['00','Start Here','Product brief, required screens, fixed content and flows'],['01','Foundations','Brand colors and typography'],['02','Components','Basic interface elements'],['04','Media','Images, logos, illustrations and icons']].map(([n,title,detail]) => <div><span class="page-icon">▱</span><span><strong><span>{n}</span> {title}</strong><small>{detail}</small></span></div>)}</div><div class="starter-footer"><span>Original reference layouts withheld</span><span class="open-badge">Open design</span></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="coverage-strip"><div><strong>8 domains</strong><p>Diverse product contexts</p></div><div><strong>12 screen categories</strong><p>Different interface functions</p></div><div><strong>9.3 screens / task</strong><p>Multi-screen design coherence</p></div><button type="button" class="button button-white" data-figure="classic.png" data-caption="Benchmark composition: application domains and required screen categories">Explore coverage</button></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="process" class="section page-width">
        <div class="section-top"><div><p class="eyebrow">FROM REQUIREMENTS TO AN EDITABLE ARTIFACT</p><h2>Watch the design take shape.</h2></div><p class="section-aside">A recorded Foodgo example.<br />Selected screenshot observations, in sequence.</p></div>
        <div class="process-viewer">
          <div class="process-sidebar"><span class="small-label">CLAUDE OPUS 5.5 · FOODGO</span><div class="process-steps">{steps.map((step,i) => <button type="button" class={`process-step ${i===0?'active':''}`} data-step={i} aria-pressed={i===0?'true':'false'}><span class="step-marker">{i===0?'✓':'◇'}</span><span><strong>{step.title}</strong><small>{step.tool}</small></span></button>)}</div><button id="replay" class="button button-dark" type="button" aria-pressed="false"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d={icons.play} /></svg><span>Play sequence</span></button></div>
          <div class="process-preview"><div class="preview-toolbar"><span>Figma screenshot observation</span><span id="step-count">1 / {steps.length}</span></div><div class="process-image"><img id="process-image" src={asset(steps[0].image)} alt={steps[0].title} loading="lazy" width="667" height="1024" /></div><div class="process-description" aria-live="polite"><strong id="step-title">{steps[0].title}</strong><p id="step-description">{steps[0].description}</p></div></div>
        </div>
        <p class="under-caption">All eleven models use the same Claude Code harness with four Figma MCP tools: <code>whoami</code>, <code>get_metadata</code>, <code>get_screenshot</code>, and <code>use_figma</code>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="evaluation" class="section evaluation-section">
        <div class="page-width"><div class="section-top"><div><p class="eyebrow">BEYOND REFERENCE SIMILARITY</p><h2>Good design has more than one dimension.</h2></div></div>
          <div class="evaluation-intro"><p>We translate professional design knowledge into <strong>22 expert-grounded rubrics across six dimensions</strong>, and compare their assessments with independent, blinded preferences from professional designers.</p><span class="score-stamp"><strong>53</strong><span>maximum<br />rubric points</span></span></div>
          <div class="dimension-grid">{dimensions.map((d,i) => <article><div class={`dimension-symbol symbol-${i}`} aria-hidden="true">{['◎','▤','⊞','◈','✳','▱'][i]}</div><h3>{d.name}</h3><p>{d.detail}</p><span>{d.criteria} rubrics <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> {d.maximum} points</span></article>)}</div>
          <div class="evaluation-tracks"><div><span class="track-label">RUBRIC ASSESSMENT</span><h3>Three judges. Three passes each.</h3><p>Each design artifact is evaluated as a whole using screenshots and Figma structure. Scores are averaged across three VLM judges and three seeded passes.</p></div><div><span class="track-label">PROFESSIONAL PREFERENCE</span><h3>Blinded comparisons. Independent judgment.</h3><p>Professional designers compare complete deliverables with model identities and automated scores concealed. Direct preferences are aggregated with Bradley–Terry.</p></div></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="results" class="section page-width">
        <div class="section-top"><div><p class="eyebrow">ELEVEN MODELS. ONE SHARED DESIGN ENVIRONMENT.</p><h2>The results, in perspective.</h2></div><a class="button button-white" href={paper} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Full results in the paper</a></div>
        <div class="result-highlights"><div><strong>0.764</strong><span>Kendall’s τ<sub>b</sub></span><p>Model-ranking agreement between human preference and rubric scores.</p></div><div><strong>76.58<span>%</span></strong><span>Pairwise agreement</span><p>Rubric-based preferences consistent with professional judgments.</p></div><div class="finding"><span class="small-label">A KEY FINDING</span><h3>Visual quality and editable structure are distinct capabilities.</h3><p>Leading models show different strengths across layout, usability, and deliverable quality.</p></div></div>
        <div class="results-toolbar"><h3>Model performance</h3><div class="table-controls"><label><span class="sr-only">Search models</span><input id="model-search" type="search" placeholder="Find a model…" /></label><label><span class="sr-only">Sort results</span><select id="result-sort"><option value="overall">Sort: Rubric score</option><option value="elo">Sort: Human Elo</option><option value="winRate">Sort: Human win rate</option></select></label></div></div>
        <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Model results table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Rank</th><th scope="col">Model</th><th scope="col">Rubric score</th><th scope="col">Human Elo</th><th scope="col">Human win rate</th></tr></thead><tbody id="model-rows">{models.map((m,i) => <tr><td class="rank">{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</td><th scope="row">{m.name}{i===0&&<span class="best-badge">Top rubric score</span>}</th><td class="score-cell"><div><span class="score-bar" style={`width:${m.overall}%`}></span><strong>{m.overall.toFixed(2)}<small>%</small></strong></div></td><td>{m.elo.toFixed(2)}</td><td>{m.winRate.toFixed(2)}%</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p id="no-models" class="empty-state" hidden>No matching models. Try another name.</p>
        <p class="table-note">Rubric scores: paper results on 84 tasks, normalized by the 53-point maximum. The overall score weights dimensions by their maximum points. Human preference columns report the paper’s Arena results.</p>
        <div class="profile-panel"><div class="profile-copy"><p class="eyebrow">LOOK PAST THE OVERALL SCORE</p><h3>Compare design strengths.</h3><p>Each axis is a normalized dimension score. Explore where models converge, and where their design practices differ.</p><div class="profile-selectors"><label><span><i class="mini-swatch purple"></i> Model A</span><select id="profile-a">{models.map((m,i)=><option value={i} selected={i===0}>{m.name}</option>)}</select></label><label><span><i class="mini-swatch coral"></i> Model B</span><select id="profile-b">{models.map((m,i)=><option value={i} selected={i===1}>{m.name}</option>)}</select></label></div></div><div class="radar-wrap"><svg id="radar" viewBox="0 0 460 370" role="img" aria-label="Six-dimension comparison chart"></svg><p class="radar-caption">Normalized rubric scores (%) · Higher is better</p></div><div id="profile-values" class="profile-values" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
        <div class="paper-insights"><button type="button" data-figure="trade-off.png" data-caption="Quality–efficiency trade-offs reported in the paper"><span class="small-label">QUALITY & EFFICIENCY</span><h3>More time or cost does not guarantee better design.</h3><span class="text-link">Explore the paper’s trade-off figure</span></button><button type="button" data-figure="oloha-native-hierarchy.png" data-caption="Native Figma structure example from the paper"><span class="small-label">THE NATIVE ARTIFACT</span><h3>A polished screen is only part of the deliverable.</h3><span class="text-link">Inspect the structure example</span></button></div>
      </section>

      <section id="paper" class="section paper-section"><div class="page-width paper-layout"><div><p class="eyebrow">THE PAPER</p><h2>FigmaEval</h2><p class="paper-full-title">A Benchmark for Evaluating AI Agents in Professional UI Design</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button button-primary" href={project.arxiv || paper} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{project.arxiv?'Read on arXiv':'Read paper PDF'}</a><a class="button button-white" href={project.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore the code</a></div></div><div class="abstract"><h3>Abstract</h3><p>Recent advances in AI agents have enabled autonomous UI design using professional tools such as Figma, yet evaluating their design capabilities remains challenging due to the open-ended nature of design tasks and the absence of unique ground truths.</p><p>FigmaEval comprises 84 tasks and 783 screens derived from real-world Figma design files. It standardizes requirements and design resources while leaving layout, visual hierarchy, and component composition open. Twenty-two expert-grounded rubrics and blinded professional comparisons assess design quality beyond reference similarity.</p><p>Evaluating eleven models reveals substantial agreement between rubric-based assessments and human preferences, alongside distinct design practices and quality–efficiency trade-offs.</p></div></div><div class="page-width citation-block"><div><h3>Cite this work</h3><button id="copy-citation" type="button">Copy BibTeX</button></div><pre><code id="citation">{citation}</code></pre><p id="copy-status" class="sr-only" aria-live="polite"></p></div></section>
    </main>
    <footer class="page-width site-footer"><a href="#" class="footer-brand">FigmaEval<span>.</span></a><p>Professional UI design. Open-ended evaluation.</p><a href="https://github.com/RomanHauksson/academic-project-astro-template" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Adapted from the Academic Project Astro Template</a></footer>
    <dialog id="image-dialog" aria-labelledby="dialog-caption"><div class="dialog-toolbar"><p id="dialog-caption"></p><button id="close-dialog" type="button" aria-label="Close enlarged image">×</button></div><div id="dialog-content"></div></dialog>
    <script type="application/json" id="page-data" set:html={JSON.stringify({ models, dimensions, tasks, steps, base }).replace(/</g, '\\u003c')} />
    <script>
      import '../scripts/interactions';
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
